INT. DRY RIVERBED SALOON - LATE AFTERNOON

A saloon built on stilts over a cracked, sun-bleached riverbed. Heat bars the window slats. A ceiling fan turns lazily. The bar is a single long plank on barrels.

EZEKIEL "ZEKE" HOLLOWAY (50s, bartender, apron stained to the color of old tea) polishes a glass that will never be clean. In the corner, MADAME ODALYS RENFRO-TALL (60s, black lace gloves, parasol closed on her lap) plays solitaire. At the far table, three MEN sit with their hats low: CASSIUS BLAINE (40s, scar through one eyebrow), LOOSE-JOINTED LEON (30s, rolling a cigarette that won't hold), and BOY WILLIAMS (18, still wearing a school tie under his duster).

A telegraph key chatters on the wall behind the bar. Zeke ignores it.

ZEKE
(to the room)
Gents. Madame. We close at five.

CASSIUS
It's four-fifteen.

ZEKE
I like to be ahead.

LEON
(to himself)
Ahead of what?

BOY WILLIAMS
Cassius, is it true the river used to run right through here?

CASSIUS
Boy, that's what the old men said.

BOY WILLIAMS
Why'd it stop?

CASSIUS
Because somebody asked it too many questions.

The key chatters again. Madame Renfro-Tall lifts her head.

ODALYS
Zeke, dear, that's the third time.

ZEKE
I know.

ODALYS
Perhaps you should answer.

Zeke sets the glass down. He walks to the key, cocks his head, reads the dashes with his lips moving.

ZEKE
(reading)
"Marshal... arriving... on... four... forty."

He turns slowly. The three men at the table have gone very still. Leon's cigarette falls apart on the tabletop.

CASSIUS
Zeke.

ZEKE
I didn't send for him.

CASSIUS
Somebody did.

All eyes turn to Madame Renfro-Tall. She smooths a card, lays it down, and smiles with a calm that has seen five governors come and go.

ODALYS
Well, someone had to.

Leon shoves back his chair. Boy Williams rises half an inch and sits again, because Cassius has put a hand on his arm.

LEON
(muttering)
Knew it. Knew it was the old lady.

ODALYS
Dear Leon, you've never known anything, and it's what makes you restful.

A long beat. The fan turns. Outside, far off, a horse whinnies.

ZEKE
(to the room, quiet)
Nobody draws until I say.

CASSIUS
(to Zeke)
You say?

ZEKE
It's my bar.

Boy Williams swallows. He looks at the door, at the window, at Cassius's hand. Dust motes drift down in the light.

BOY WILLIAMS
(whispering to himself)
Four-forty. Four-forty.

Cassius takes his hand off the boy's arm. He lifts his glass, finds it empty, and sets it down gently.

CASSIUS
Zeke, I'll have another.

ZEKE
You'll have water.

CASSIUS
There isn't any water.

ZEKE
(a faint smile)
Then you'll have what we've got.

He pours something amber into the glass. The telegraph key falls silent. Madame Renfro-Tall plays a red seven on a black eight.

MARSHAL TREVELYAN (O.S.)
Zeke Holloway! Step out onto the porch!

Zeke lays down the bottle. Nobody breathes.
